Germany's Energiewende programme demonstrates that a major industrial economy can decarbonise its grid without blackouts — renewables supplied 59% of German electricity in 2023.
Grid stability concerns are addressed by geographic diversity: when the sun isn't shining in one region, wind is blowing in another, and interconnected grids balance supply automatically.
Fossil fuels are finite — the IEA estimates current proven reserves would be exhausted within decades at current consumption rates; renewables have no such constraint.
Pumped hydro, already the world's largest form of energy storage, provides seasonal storage for renewable surplus; new technologies like iron-air batteries extend this further.
The US, China, India, and EU all have binding commitments to reach 80%+ clean electricity by 2035–2040 — the policy momentum is irreversible.
